  "derivation": "Foundation 31 TouchCollection and its nested Enumerator. Contract rows come from the pinned public metadata and from docs/generated/xna-il-inventory.json, which tools/api_compat/build_il_inventory.rb derives from Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Input.Touch.dll (SHA-256 b0585224…) admitted by exact hash. Mapping rows encode what that assembly IL establishes: the struct declares eight TouchLocation slots and an int32 count, so the constructor accepts eight elements and throws ArgumentOutOfRangeException(touches) for nine and ArgumentNullException(touches) for null; isConnected is the literal true; each element is expanded through TouchLocation.TryGetPreviousLocation, using TouchLocationState.Invalid and a zero previous position when there is none; IsReadOnly is the literal true and six members are a two-instruction newobj NotSupportedException; throw, projected to CNA::Runtime::NotSupportedError, which is a StandardError and not a ScriptError; the indexer constructs ArgumentOutOfRangeException(index) in its own IL, which maps to RangeError; IndexOf scans with op_Equality over all seven fields, so a TouchLocation that Equals answers true for is still not found; Contains is that scan negated; CopyTo throws ArgumentNullException(array) once and ArgumentOutOfRangeException(arrayIndex) twice; FindById fills the CLR default TouchLocation on the false branch; and the enumerator starts at -1, forwards Current to the indexer with no guard, clamps position to Count, and has a bare-ret Dispose. None is snapshotted from the cna-ruby implementation.",
